/**
 * A synchronous hold on Moodle's pending-JavaScript counter, the one Behat waits on.
 *
 * core/ajax holds the counter only while a request is in flight. A chain of requests separated by
 * a React render (tier 1 settles, state changes, an effect then asks for the strip) leaves a gap in
 * which nothing is pending, and Behat reads the page as ready before the strip exists. core/pending
 * would close the gap, but loading it through RequireJS opens another; M.util.js_pending, which
 * core/pending itself calls, is on every page and takes effect at once.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/pending
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
/**
 * The one place that knows a React component cannot import an AMD module.
 *
 * The import map Moodle serves has six keys and none of them is AMD: the prefix
 * `@moodle/lms/`, the design system, and react and react-dom with a subpath key each
 * (`lib/classes/output/requirements/import_map.php`, add_standard_imports).
 * A bare import of `core/ajax` from a .tsx is therefore a resolution failure
 * at runtime, and react_autoinit turns that into a console.error and a
 * component that never mounts - a blank region, not an error anyone sees.
 *
 * RequireJS is loaded on every Moodle page by a classic script, and every
 * React component is reached through a deferred module script, so the global
 * is always defined before a component runs. Core's own ESM reaches for page
 * globals the same way: lib/js/esm/src/profiler.ts reads window.M.cfg.jsrev.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/amd
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
/**
 * The only module that talks to the server.
 *
 * The core/ajax and core_user/repository modules are AMD, which an ES module cannot import,
 * so they are loaded through the bridge in amd.ts; every web service call and preference
 * write of the client goes through here.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/repository
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
/**
 * Up next ("Em seguida", ADR-016): what closes or starts in the next hours, drawn inside the calendar
 * between its head and tier 1, and the ladder whose highest rung rings Today.
 *
 * Two halves. useUpNext() is the asking: it reads the gate the counts or the open strip already carry
 * (calendar.ts gateOpens(), the twin of strip::gate_opens()) and asks get_soon only when it opens, so a
 * calm day costs no request; otherwise it sets one timer to the instant the gate would open
 * (gateNext()). An answer replaces the view's gate lists with the pruned ones it carries and sets the
 * offset of the server's clock, which every countdown follows. One timer aligned to the minute
 * re-evaluates the ladder while an answer holds items, with no request per tick. The timer lives in
 * the region (UpNext), not in the hook: each minute re-renders the region alone, and the strip, which
 * hosts the hook, re-renders only when the rows' count or the level changes (advance()). The timer
 * stops while the page is hidden and evaluates at once when it is shown. A page hidden for 15
 * minutes or more asks again through the gate when it is shown, and a timer that fired while it was
 * hidden asks then. Nothing else polls (decision 5).
 *
 * UpNext is the region: at most two rows in time order, the line of the rest with the way to today's
 * card, the hide button and, while hidden, the quiet line in its place (decisions 1 and 7). It opens
 * from no height to its own (styles.css, grid rows from 0fr to 1fr), so tier 1 slides rather than
 * jumps, and at once under reduced motion. A polite live region (the strip's own) says once when the
 * highest level rises, never on the first paint of the region and never per minute (decision 6).
 *
 * Its strings are the calendar props' own (strings.*), as the strip's are.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/UpNext
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
/**
 * The client's two uses of core/notification: reporting a failure and asking before an action.
 *
 * Both settle without rejecting. They are awaited from click handlers and effects that have no
 * catch of their own, and the module they load is an AMD module reached through RequireJS (see
 * amd.ts), whose load can fail; a rejection there would surface as an unhandled one. A failure
 * to report is not itself reported: the module that would say it is the one that did not load.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/notify
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
/**
 * The block_compass_calendar preference as the page holds it: every member, whoever wrote it last.
 *
 * Two writers share one JSON value: the calendar strip (view, filter, relevance, open, and Up next's
 * soon) and the open-courses line (news, ADR-017 decision 9). Core's route replaces the whole value on
 * every write, so a writer that sent only its own members would drop the other's: the line's hide would
 * reset the strip, and a toggle of the strip would bring a hidden line back. So every write goes through
 * here, merged over what the page last held, seeded once from the shell's props (Block.tsx).
 *
 * A failed write keeps the members on this page, as the strip's remember() does; a writer that puts its
 * own member back says so with putBack(), which writes nothing.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/calendarpref
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
/**
 * The calendar strip above tier 1 (ADR-014): a row of days weighed by what each holds, and a
 * day's items on demand; closed, one line of plain counts by period (amendment 11).
 *
 * Mounted by Block only when the shell shipped the calendar props, so a site with the strip off
 * mounts nothing and asks nothing (decision 1). The heading holds a disclosure toggle; whether the
 * strip starts open is the viewer's remembered toggle, or the site's calendar_start, shipped with
 * the props, so the first paint reserves the shape it keeps: the row's skeleton open, the line's
 * closed. Its request leaves only when tier 1's request has settled, answered or failed: Block hands
 * the turn down (decision 2). Closed, that request is get_strip_counts and nothing else: no
 * get_strip, no get_day. With the open-courses line on, that first read travels in one request beside
 * get_news (ADR-017 amendment 1): the block starts the pair and hands the strip its share as batched, which
 * the strip awaits in place of its own first ask, under its own sequence number; a read the batch did not
 * answer is asked again alone, and every later ask is the strip's own. The strip keeps the block's
 * openref current, so the pair leaves with the strip's first read for the shape it has then. Opening
 * asks get_strip once; closing asks nothing, the line being summed
 * from the payload in hand (calendar.ts, countsOf()). Either answer carries Up next's gate (ADR-016
 * decision 3), and get_soon is asked only when it opens (UpNext.tsx, useUpNext()); the region is the
 * section's last child, under the line or the row, so it sits between the calendar's head and tier 1
 * in either shape and stays mounted across a toggle, and its highest rung rings today's chip or disc.
 * Every write of the preference carries Up next's hide (the soon member) along, and the open-courses
 * line's (news) through calendarpref.ts, which holds every member. The strip tells the block what the
 * line's weight is (ADR-017 decision 7) from what it holds, never with a request: whether its counts
 * have settled, whether they count work late, today or tomorrow, and whether Up next shows. The toggle is
 * written to the preference at once and put back, with a notice, if the write fails. Each zone loads,
 * fails and retries on its own, so a failed strip never blanks the cards; a request that failed is
 * asked again by its own Try again, or by tier 1's.
 *
 * The row is one native horizontal scroller holding the whole window, which the payload already
 * carries, so a scroll costs no request; it snaps per day, and nothing hijacks the wheel or drags it
 * (ADR-014 amendment 12). It opens on its home position, five past days before today (three on a
 * narrow block), placed before paint and never on a frame, which a hidden tab does not run. Two
 * arrows float over the row's ends and move whole weeks, Today returns home, and the month follows
 * the days wholly in view. Under the row, the window's map is a pointer scrubber (aria-hidden: the row
 * does all of it by keyboard). Below 640 px of block width a half day peeks, there are no arrows and no
 * map, and a day opens as a bottom sheet.
 *
 * A day opens after 120 ms of pointing, 40 ms when moving from an open day, and only when it is wholly
 * in view; a press or keyboard focus on a peeking day scrolls it into view first. It closes 200 ms after
 * the pointer leaves the strip and its card, which is rendered once beside the scroller (a scroller
 * clips its children on both axes) and placed under its day. A user's scroll closes a hovered day, and
 * a pinned one once its day leaves the view; the strip's own scrolls close nothing and hold Moodle's
 * pending counter until they settle, so Behat never clicks mid-animation. A click pins a day, Escape
 * closes it, focus opens it as hovering does, and the window is one tab stop moved with the arrow keys,
 * Page Up and Down, Home and End. Days fetched are kept for the page, keyed by view and date; only the
 * day after a pointed day is prefetched, and only when it holds something; answers are matched by
 * sequence number, never cancelled.
 *
 * Its strings are the calendar props' own, read as strings.*, never the block's labels: the
 * strip's props do not exist on a site with it off.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/CalendarStrip
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
/**
 * Open for enrolment ("Inscrições abertas", ADR-017): the courses the viewer could join, starting in the
 * band, grouped by reference category, drawn as option B of docs/mockup-new-courses/.
 *
 * Two halves, as Up next. useNews() is the asking, held by the block so a move of the line keeps its
 * answer: get_news once, when the block says it may (after tier 1 has settled), never on a timer and never
 * again on visibilitychange. With the calendar on, that once is the block's batch (ADR-017 amendment 1): the
 * news left in one request with the strip's first read, and useNews() awaits the share it is handed in place
 * of its own ask, under its own sequence number; the line is placed once the strip's answer is in, so the
 * weight is known. A read the batch did not answer is asked again alone.
 * Nothing is asked while the shell says the line is hidden for this sign-in. A failure offers its own Try
 * again, and tier 1's asks again too. The × hides the line at once and writes the news member through
 * calendarpref.ts, which carries every other member of the preference; a failed write puts it back with
 * news_hideerror.
 *
 * OpenCourses is the drawing, in the shape the block chose (decision 7), all three in one panel (round 2,
 * docs/mockup-new-courses/Rodada2.dc.html): the full line (the eyebrow and the summary over a head, up to
 * three groups as links on a row of their own, "+N", and a labelled close), the slim panel with "See", or
 * tier 1's empty state as tiles. "See" and "+N" open the groups in a popover, a dialog that takes the keyboard and
 * gives it back, closed by Escape and by a press outside it; below 640 px of the block's width it is a
 * bottom sheet over a scrim. Nothing is drawn when no course counts: no line, no zero, no reserved space.
 * The line grows in from no height (styles.css, grid rows from 0fr to 1fr, Up next's way), at once under
 * reduced motion.
 *
 * Its strings are the line's own props (news.strings), never the block's labels. The band's last day is
 * written from lang strings (calendar.ts dayMonth()), the calendar's when it renders here, else the ones
 * the shell ships beside the line's.
 *
 * Crests and kinds (ADR-019 decisions 2, 3 and 6, docs/mockup-category-crests/): a group whose category a
 * theme crested draws the first crest beside its name, in the pill (alt empty: the name is beside it), in
 * the popover's rows and in the tiles, where a group without one keeps an empty slot while some group has
 * one. With a theme naming kinds, the summary and the second lines count courses and events apart; with
 * the default kind alone every text reads as before. The pill's name is cut at 12.5 rem and its whole text
 * is in GroupTip, the block's own tooltip, never core's Bootstrap one and never a title: aria-hidden (the
 * pill's accessible name says it all), opened by a hover held 300 ms or at once by keyboard focus, kept
 * while the pointer is on it, closed by Escape without moving focus and by leaving both; it hangs from the
 * anchor beside the clip, above the pill or below it when there is no room, inside the block's width. A tile
 * opens the same tooltip, and only while its name or second line is cut: the tile shows the rest itself.
 * No tooltip below 640 px of the block, where the pill takes the row and a pill's or tile's name wraps to
 * two lines.
 *
 * @module     block_compass/OpenCourses
 * @copyright  2026 Anderson Blaine
 * @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later
 */
